Round-1 reads (forensics reads.py) show what the digest failed to carry:
- repo.md in 9 of 10 r1 sessions, behind the digest's own truncation marker;
- plan.md for the upcoming phase's tasks (S9 grepped "chat"; its 421-char spec was in the task title while the digest showed only "Active phase: (none)");
- memory.md (memories never render);
- decisions.md greps for rules the constraint cap had hidden (S9 hid D20; the cap drops the newest first).
Relevance forensics: every planted decision (A1–A6) rendered in Standing constraints, while recent decisions (~1.4k), the rejected ledger (up to 2.1k), the last summary (~1.2k) and repo memory (1.5k) bore almost no relation to the session's decisions.
Putting the task first and the rules last uses both ends of the context (lost-in-the-middle; Anthropic's query-last guidance). Relevance ranking drops the least relevant rules instead of the newest.
Frozen predictions: R2-P10 ≤1 raw .sofar read per session; M3 unchanged or better.

payload.chose:
1.3 CONTRACT (digest composition), replacing r1-fixes D12's volatility order in renderStatus only (sofar status and plan.md unchanged). HARD CAP 6,000 chars (was 10,000). ORDER:
(1) title, Goal (≤400);
(2) NEXT TASK, the spec first: the active phase's active task, else its first pending or blocked task, else the first such task of the first open phase. Its title whole up to 1,000 chars, then its phase line and the phase's other open tasks (id + title ≤80, max 6);
(3) Next action and its drift lines (parallel write-backs, staleness, notes, blocked, concurrent edits), as today;
(4) Last session (≤600, pointer when clipped); Driven; unwritten-session lines;
(5) Phases + Progress, compact;
(6) MEMORY: this record's in-force memory_promoted entries, ranked by relevance to the focus text (next task title + phase name + next action; ties newest first), whole while they fit the budget, then one-line heads (≤90), then `…and N more in memory.md`;
(7) Repo memory (≤600): the init stub preamble is stripped, and a bullet naming an M handle of this record that §6 rendered is dropped;
(8) Recent decisions (last 5 in force) and Earlier rejected approaches with MINUTIAE DROPPED: each chose/over cut at its first `; `, ` — `, `: ` or ` (` past 24 chars, then clipped (chose 90, over 70). A ruled decision shows `(rule below)` and its chose head only;
(9) Next ids, adjacency, Session, Git, notices;
(10) STANDING CONSTRAINTS LAST, ranked by relevance to the same focus text (ties newest first), whole entries under the 2,000 budget, the least relevant dropping with the pointer;
(11) Read-back, footer.
BUDGET: fixed sections render as budgeted. The yielding sections take what the cap leaves, in this order of precedence: memory, last session, decisions window, repo memory, rejected ledger. So the rules and the read-back are never the tail cut. Relevance = distinct shared terms via core/lexicon's tokenizer. Pure and deterministic.

